> 3. Evaluate the usefulness of chaotic artifacts.

I notice that no one else went for this so I will.

Grimtooth, Orcrist, Sting, Stormbringer.  Quest artifacts
Longbow of Dianna and Master Key of Thievery.

Longbow of Dianna - Grants reflection when wielded but that's
too unreliable.  Makes its own arrows so it would be great for
any class that can reach multifire in bow/arrow.  In other words
if you're a Ranger or Samurai it's great, if your a Caveman at
skilled quite nice.  Not a lot of chaotic samurai or cavemen
wandering around, are there?  So it is extremely specific to
rangers.

Master Key of Thievery - Grants warning and half physical damage.
Warning is available through a ring so big deal.  Half physical
damage is nice (also Orb of Weight) but several others give half
spell damage and that's even better.  Light enough to carry and
I generally keep a key in the bag anyways.  Worth carrying but
otherwise so what a lot of the time.

Stormbringer - A lot of people undervalue Stormy and I find
myself in the minority valuing it.  It's disadvantage of
attacking peacefulls is a serious problem so be sure to wield
something else when in a shrine.  Since Stormy drains levels
from nearly all monsters, that means melee combat will almost
always defeat any one monster no matter how powerfull.  Manage
to stay out of a crowd and your opponents are toast.  There's
a complaint that Stormy turns to junk when you hit the planes
because the riders resist level drain.  Okay, but the riders
aren't proof to any +6 or +7 melee weapon so they tend to fall
anyways.  And on Astral it's a swipe or two and run for the
altar anyways.  Fighting on Astral is mostly to clear a path to
advance anyways.  Stormy does not zap items in the monster's
inventory so in that sense it beats Frosty, Mojo and Firey.

Orcrist - It may be junk but it has one advantage - A chaotic
can name it before the first sacfest and thus be guaranteed
some other chaotic artifact as the first gift (unaligned
artifacts are only second and subsequent gifts).  But among
junk Orcrist isn't *that* bad.  Elven broadsword is one of the
better non-artifact weapons.

Sting - Daggers rule.  Daggers that don't stack make figuring
out what to wield and what to throw easy.  Being able to see
all of the orcs on a level doesn't suck until you're XP=9 or
so.  Did I mention that daggers rule?  Okay, well daggers that
are immune to rust monsters and black puddings rule even better.
Sting also has the same advantage as Orcrist - A chaotic can
name it before the first sacfest and thus be guaranteed some
other chaotic artifact as the first gift.  Being able to cut
through all webs ends up being a so-what most of the game.

Grimtooth - Daggers rule.  Daggers that don't stack make
figuring out what to wield and what to throw easy.  Daggers
that come straight from the manufacturer already rustproofed
make rust monsters and black puddings not so much of a
problem.  Daggers that have additional damage against *all*
monsters don't suck.  Grimtooth may not compete with Greyswandir
but it beats most non-artifact weapons a lot of the time.

More than considering individual chaotic artifacts, note
that they form a team in a way that other alignments don't.
An elven non-wizard chaotic can name Sting and Orcrist and
be gauranteed to get Stormy as the first gift (elves don't
get orcish artifacts).  Since I value Stormy over anything but
Greyswandir and maybe Frosty this is great.  And any other
chaotic will get either an okay general purpose missile that
is a superior daggers-rule or one of the best artifacts (in
my admittedly minority opinion).



